    33	"There," she said. "That's everything I know."
    35	"It's a lot," said Brom, from the door.
    37	"It's everything I know about the wrong country." She had reached the right-hand end. "Every line on this table was written inside. Registry stone, academy stone, copies of copies from archives with doors on them. All of it is *before the Line*. I've walked eight weeks in a place where nothing is verified and every cart that passes me has a man on it who knows something, and I have not got one line out of any of them I could set on this table under a slip." She looked at the hands. "And I couldn't write it if I had."
    39	"You've got the woman's," Lira said, from the bench by the door, the stick across her knees. "Dusk. Out of the water. River's their colour. That's three lines."
    41	"That's lore. It's good lore. It kept us alive. It's under a slip." Karis nodded down the plank at a bundle at the far end, thin, one page. "That's the shelf. One page, dictated, in his hand. There's no *record* out here. There are people who know things and a road, and the people get on the road and go, and what they know goes with them." She said it without heat, at the pitch she used for a finding. "I'm a researcher in a country that keeps nothing. I'd like to write that down, and I can't hold the pen."
    53	"You mean the quiet ground? Everybody knows the quiet ground. Nobody goes in."
    61	"That," she said, "is a source." She closed the book on it. "Not a good one. The first."
    83	"At Norhold," she said, "I told them I wanted to become something they hadn't seen yet."
    87	"They wrote it down. It was the thing you were supposed to say, and I meant it, and they wrote it in the column for things people say." She turned the foot the other way. "The Silver bracket was the ladder. Every rung has a number on it and the number is how you know you've climbed, and a panel at the top of every flight tells you what you've become since the last one. I walked out of it." A pause, with the fire in it. "On purpose, at full price, and I'd do it again. I'm not saying anything about that."
    89	"You're saying something," said Brom.
    91	"I'm saying that on the bar I was fast." She put the foot down. "Faster than I've been with Wind. I don't know if that's true. Nobody was counting. I know it's what it felt like, and I know I did it on a floor I can't run on anymore, and the next time I run there's going to be nobody at the end of it with a number." She found the exact sentence and said it to the fire. "So what am I becoming, exactly, if nobody's measuring?"
    95	"Fast," said Brom, eventually, because it was the word he had.
    97	"I know I'm fast. I was fast at fourteen. Fast was the *floor*." She reached for the boot. "Something they haven't seen yet, I said. Well. They haven't seen it. They're not here to see it. That isn't the same as becoming it, and I don't know what would be."
   101	"Write it when you can."
   113	"Thornwater," he said, to the page. "Read it at the plank a week ago. Saying's tonight, at the book, if you want to hear it said."
   115	"We do," said Cael.
   117	"Then tonight." The pen did not stop.
   127	"I keep waiting for someone to ask for my report."
   131	"Fifteen years," Seln said. "Every road, every ford, every door. Somebody asked. Somebody always asked; that was the whole of it; the asking was the job and I was the answer, and I never liked one of them." He took the finger off the slip. "Eight weeks. I've walked every road out of two places. Nobody's asked."
   133	"Is there one?"
   135	"There's always one."
   137	"Then—"
   139	"No." Seln turned, finally. "Not if you ask. That's the thing I'm finding out. I don't want to be asked. I've been asked. I wanted to see what I did with it when nobody did." He looked at the board a moment longer. "It turns out I take the well, and I walk it first, and I say nothing. It's the same craft. It weighs less." He went back to the slips, and that was the report.
   147	"What does the board pay a man with no Path at all."
   151	"Same as anyone," he said, at the pitch he said a price. "What'd he do?"
